    type: 'MCQ',
    topic: 'Data Interpretation - Charts & Graphs',
    difficulty: 'Hard',
    context: "The chart below shows the price data for seven shares \u2013 A, B, C, D, E, F, and G\nas a candlestick plot for a particular day. The vertical axis shows the price of the share\nin rupees. A share whose closing price (price at the end of the day) is more than its\nopening price (price at the start of the day) is called a bullish share; otherwise, it is\ncalled a bearish share. All bullish and bearish shares are shown in green and red colour\nrespectively.\n\n![Diagram](/images/questions/dilr24-s1_ctx4_32842_332818_0_25976758_kitkat_dilr_nd_jpg.jpg)",
    question: "What would have been the percentage wealth gain for a trader, who bought equal numbers of\r\n                                all bullish shares at opening price and sold them at their day's high?",
    options: ["100%", "80%", "72%", "50%"],
    correctAnswer: 1,
    explanation: "DAY HIGH\nDAY LOW\nOPENING\nCLOSING\n\nA\n2400\n1200\n2200\n1800\n\nB\n2000\n1400\n2000\n1700\n\nC\n1400\n800\n800\n1200\n\nD\n1200\n300\n500\n1000\n\nE\n1400\n1100\n1300\n1100\n\nF\n2000\n1200\n1800\n1600\n\nG\n1900\n1000\n1200\n1700\n\nSolution:\n\nA share whose closing price (price at the end of the day) is more than its opening price\n(price at the start of the day) is called a bullish share. Bullish shares on that day are C, D, and G.\n\nOpening price of bullish shares (C, D, and G) together = 800 + 500 + 1200 = 2500.\n\nDay\u2019s high price of bullish shares (C, D, and G) together = 1400 + 1200 + 1900 = 4500.\n\nPercentage wealth gain = ((4500 - 2500) / 2500) * 100 = (2000 / 2500) * 100 = 80%."
